  "name": "SCRUB TYPHUS(TSUTSUGAMUSHI) ANTIBODY IgM,EIA",
  "code": "2041",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 99.973%; height: 133.182px;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 50.064%;\"><col style=\"width: 50.064%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.2955px;\">\n<td>RESULT (INDEX)&nbsp;</td>\n<td>REMARKS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.2955px;\">\n<td>&lt;0.9&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.2955px;\">\n<td>0.9 - 1.1</td>\n<td>Equivocal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.2955px;\">\n<td>&gt;1.1&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>1. Positive result indicates presence of IgM antibody to Orientia tsutsugamushi suggestive of primary<br>infection. Evaluation by supplemental test like Immunofluorescence assay is suggested for IgM positive<br>cases.<br>2. Negative result indicates absence of IgM antibody to Orientia tsutsugamushi. Patients with negative<br>results in suspected early disease should be re-tested after 2-3 weeks.<br>3. Equivocal result indicates low levels of antibodies hence should be re-tested after 2-3 weeks.<br>4. False positive results can be obtained due to cross reaction with Epstein-Barr virus, Brucella, Dengue<br>Virus.<br>5. False negative results may be seen in immunocompromised patients.<br>6. Test conducted on serum.</p>",
